# The scroll scene: the scroll viewport's contract — content
# overflows the visible extent (both readings geometry), scroll_end
# drives the toolkit's REAL scrolling API, expect_at_end reads the
# content's end edge back, and the trailing click proves the
# scrolled-to content is live (the bottom button writes the status
# label at the top).
# A SCROLL SPANS ITS PARENT'S CROSS AXIS (ruled 2026-09-02): the
# viewport is a region, not content, and a user pans anywhere across it.
# Every backend hugged the 29 labels' width until the iOS driver's real
# pans found a 79pt strip in a 375pt window (docs/traps.md); the widget
# half of the breadth question, read from geometry on all five lanes.
expect_breadth scroll#0
expect_overflow scroll#0
expect label#0 "at top"
scroll_end scroll#0
expect_at_end scroll#0
click button#0
expect label#0 "bottom clicked"
